================================================================================
TOPIC 1 REPORT — GENERATORS AND ITERATORS
================================================================================

CORE PURPOSE
------------
Enable lazy, memory-efficient data processing. Instead of loading entire
datasets into memory, generators produce values one at a time on demand —
the foundation of every production ML data pipeline.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

KEY CONCEPTS
------------

YIELD
  - Turns a function into a generator function
  - Calling a generator function returns a generator object — nothing executes yet
  - Execution pauses at yield, hands the value to the caller, resumes on next()
  - Local state (variables, file handles, position) is frozen between yields

LAZY EVALUATION
  - Nothing executes until something pulls from the iterator
  - The entire pipeline fires on demand, one item at a time, all the way to source
  - Enables processing of datasets too large to fit in memory

THE ITERATOR PROTOCOL
  - __iter__ — returns the iterator object (often self)
  - __next__ — returns the next value, raises StopIteration when exhausted
  - Any object implementing both is an iterator
  - Any object implementing only __iter__ is an iterable

ITERABLE VS ITERATOR
  - Iterable: has __iter__, returns an iterator when called
  - Iterator: has both __iter__ and __next__, holds iteration state
  - Lists, dicts, files are iterables — not iterators
  - Generators are iterators — they hold their own state
  - Making __iter__ a generator (with yield inside it) makes a class
    iterable-but-not-iterator — safe to loop over multiple times,
    each loop gets a fresh independent generator

GENERATOR EXPRESSIONS
  - Lazy equivalent of list comprehensions
  - (expr for item in iterable if condition)
  - Can be chained — each stage wraps the previous lazily
  - Nothing materialises until something iterates the final expression

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

THE THREE PIPELINE PRIMITIVES
------------------------------
  map    — transform each element. One item in, one item out.
  filter — keep or discard each element. One item in, zero or one items out.
  reduce — collapse many elements into one. Many items in, one item out.
           (batch() is a mild form of reduce)

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

CRITICAL RULES AND GOTCHAS
---------------------------

BOUNDARY CONDITIONS
  - Use >= not > and not == for iterator stop checks
  - > allows one extra iteration — causes IndexError on final step
  - == is brittle — breaks if index ever overshoots (e.g. subclass behaviour)
  - >= is defensive and correct in all cases

.extend() VS .append() — DATA SHAPE
  - .extend() unpacks an iterable — many items added per step
    → accumulator can overshoot batch_size in one step
    → requires >= boundary check and slice-and-carry overflow logic
  - .append() adds one item per step — accumulator grows by exactly 1
    → == boundary check is safe, no overflow possible
  - This is not a style choice — it determines the shape of your output data
    and is a real source of dimension errors in ML pipelines

MUTATION HAZARD
  - random.shuffle() mutates in place — always shuffle a copy, not self.files
  - Yielding a list and then clearing it gives the caller an empty list —
    yield a slice (batch[:n]) to give the caller an independent copy

IMPORT PLACEMENT
  - Imports inside methods hide dependencies and are not idiomatic
  - Always import at the top of the file

RETURN INSIDE A GENERATOR
  - return inside a generator raises StopIteration implicitly
  - The clean way to stop early (e.g. when max_tokens is hit)

TWO-LEVEL STRUCTURE IN PIPELINE METHODS
  - A method that returns Pipeline(generator(self.iterator)) has two levels:
    Level 1 — method call: executes immediately, builds the chain
    Level 2 — inner generator: executes lazily, only when iterated
  - If the method itself contains yield, it returns a generator not a Pipeline
    and the chain breaks — .map()/.filter() become unavailable downstream

BATCH SPANNING FILE BOUNDARIES
  - Initialise batch outside the file loop, not inside
  - Initialising inside resets the batch per file — produces short final
    batches at every file boundary, which is wrong for corpus pipelines

RESET() HAZARD
  - A reset() method on an iterator that also resets in __iter__ is redundant
  - Worse — calling reset() mid-iteration produces unpredictable repeated items
  - If you need a resettable data source, make it an iterable-not-iterator
    (yield inside __iter__) so each loop gets a fresh independent generator

